{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1498516-kinfolk-clarification","id":1498516,"title":"Kinfolk Clarification?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-12-01T03:07:52-08:00","id":1498516,"title":"Kinfolk Clarification?","content":"Kinfolk are an interesting concept.<br /> But, the combination of culture, spirit, and genetics is fascinating and a little confusing for me.<br /> I have questions...<br /> -Is Kinfolk poaching a thing? For example, would Spirals (or other rival tribes) see Kinfolk (human or wolf) as targets for breeding? <br /> -Am I correct that boosting a tribe's lupus population isn't as simple as breeding more with wolves? The odds of non-Kin wolves bearing Garou is pretty low, yeah? <br /> -But IF they do breed true, it would take a sustained and controlled breeding program over generations for those wolves to be true Kinfolk, correct?<br /> -Is there any way, short of testing someone for their reaction to the Delirium, that a human or wolf can be identified as Kinfolk?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27674-johnnyarkham","name":"JohnnyArkham","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-01T03:21:53-08:00","id":1498517,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnnyArkham</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1498516-kinfolk-clarification#post1498516\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Kinfolk are an interesting concept.<br /> -Am I correct that boosting a tribe's lupus population isn't as simple as breeding more with wolves? The odds of non-Kin wolves bearing Garou is pretty low, yeah?<br /> -But IF they do breed true, it would take a sustained and controlled breeding program over generations for those wolves to be true Kinfolk, correct?</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, it IS as simple as breeding with wolves and there is no need for breeding programs to get kin. WW/OPP basically just treat lupus as rare still because wolves were in a VERY shit situation in the 90s and the current situation is kind of breaking the motive of Red Talons to be so rarrr.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-01T05:25:18-08:00","id":1498522,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnnyArkham</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1498516-kinfolk-clarification#post1498516\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">-Is Kinfolk poaching a thing? For example, would Spirals (or other rival tribes) see Kinfolk (human or wolf) as targets for breeding?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd say that will vary wildly from tribe to tribe, even regionally perhaps, depending on each group's ideas about &quot;purity of breeding&quot;.<br /> <br /> I can definitely see tribes like Silver Fangs, Fianna or Get of Fenris being possessive of kinfolk families and lineages that they see as &quot;rightfully theirs&quot;, while Glass Walkers, Bone Gnawers or Children of Gaia should be fine with any kinfolk as viable partners, with criteria more connected to mentality/culture than lineage per se, maybe even sharing kinfolk between each other to boost numbers and connections, while certain tribes like Shadow Lords (social darwinism R' us) and Black Furies (some might prefer males of hellenic descent, for others any man who's willing to help support women against abuse might do, i guess) i imagine would stand somewhere in the middle.<br /> <br /> That said, there is at least one canon major case of kinfolk-poaching with the <b><i>line of Rurik</i></b>, the first rulers of what would eventually become the russian people, that would start as a norse line connected with the Get of Fenris, but through some centuries of &quot;backroom disputes&quot; so to speak, would be subsumed by the Silver Fangs and become of their core lineages in fact. All according to <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Rage_Across_Russia\" target=\"_blank\">Rage Across Russia</a>, at least.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 12-01-2022, 08:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-01T06:40:58-08:00","id":1498525,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1498516-kinfolk-clarification?p=1498517#post1498517\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Actually, it IS as simple as breeding with wolves and there is no need for breeding programs to get kin. WW/OPP basically just treat lupus as rare still because wolves were in a VERY shit situation in the 90s and the current situation is kind of breaking the motive of Red Talons to be so rarrr.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be fair, the wolf's population still isn't great with high estimates putting the population at a quarter of a million between all of their subspecies. Compare that to the population of humans and the fact several species are on the brink of extinction (The Red wolf is estimated to only have <b>40</b> left in the wild)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12895-mrnatas","name":"MrNatas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12895_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"180"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-01T06:48:00-08:00","id":1498526,"title":"","content":"My opinion:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnnyArkham</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1498516-kinfolk-clarification#post1498516\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">-Is Kinfolk poaching a thing? For example, would Spirals (or other rival tribes) see Kinfolk (human or wolf) as targets for breeding?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. Also, there are different levels of attachment to the garou in general or tribes specifically. By their very nature the Children of Gaia and Black Furies tend to value personal freedoms and therefore would have a tendency for any kin person to follow their passion/love. Romeo and Juliette style love stories also work great for any Fianna background. And then there is the infamous mixed-heritage Flaw for the Get of Fenris.<br /> While BSD might kindnap and rape kinfolk of other tribes, or pose as garou from an honourable tribe to kinfolk with only weak ties to garou, the other tribes would possibly not descend to such tactics - or even start to scheme, due to honour concerns. Shadow Lords not withstanding.<br /> <br /> But, most tribes would possible educate their kinfolk (that that is closer to garou, but also more traditional in general) to stay away from other tribes, romantically.<br /> <br /> As for One-Night-Stands, well, garou prior to W20 had that build in &quot;You want to fuck me&quot;-mechanics. See page 199 of revised Core. It is said that it is commonly used on humans for breeding, and doesn't exclude kinfolk (it does exclude wolves). With the knowledge that such a power exists, garou will have a clear idea what happened, if their kinfolk sons and daughters have sexual encounters with garou of other tribes without any courting or lasting affections...<br /> And they might find that offensive.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnnyArkham</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1498516-kinfolk-clarification#post1498516\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">-Am I correct that boosting a tribe's lupus population isn't as simple as breeding more with wolves? The odds of non-Kin wolves bearing Garou is pretty low, yeah?</div> </div> </div> </div>Pre-Revised there was no rule on the breed-form of a child. One would assume that the child had the breed of the non-shapeshifting parent (or is a crinos-born). Not factoring in inter-fera-offspring. Since revised, the child has always the breed of the mother.<br /> <br /> So, now only the small lupus population can parent lupus garou. While male homids could in theory shift into lupus form and impregnate a wolf, this is not common, due to the instincts of the wolf and the pack dynamic. Normally only one female wolf per pack would mate, and that with the dominant male wolf in the pack, not the garou who happens to come by.<br /> While a female lupus-born can make a conscious decision to become pregnant and probably find a male wolf to mate, this simply isn't the case for wolf kinfolk.<br /> <br /> Also, wolves only carry cubs at a specific time of the year, and while they carry more cubs in a litter than an average human pregnancy, they should still be less cubs on average, due to only one female mating.<br /> <br /> In theory all female lupus-born could be fertile in homid form all-year round, and their kids would be wolves or lupus-born, but that seems to go against instincts as well. Also, I think this is something nobody who wrote the rule into revised, thought through.<br /> <br /> In any case, garou lupus population is not easily done.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnnyArkham</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1498516-kinfolk-clarification#post1498516\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">-But IF they do breed true, it would take a sustained and controlled breeding program over generations for those wolves to be true Kinfolk, correct?</div> </div> </div> </div>What is &quot;true&quot; Kinfolk, in your words?<br /> Any wolf from a litter with a garou parent is, per definition, kinfolk. They are somewhat smarter than normal wolves, possibly, but they are essentially still animals and behave accordingly. If they themselves have litter the chances of a random garou appearing is 1% (not factoring in Pure Breed as per &quot;Unsung Heroes&quot;). Most of the litter will still be kinfolk, but some could loose that trait.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnnyArkham</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1498516-kinfolk-clarification#post1498516\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">-Is there any way, short of testing someone for their reaction to the Delirium, that a human or wolf can be identified as Kinfolk?</div> </div> </div> </div>In W20 there is still the Gift &quot;Scent of the true form&quot; that can, and could in all editions, discern Kinfolk. It is the easiest way. Although if you do not score enough successes - well, you are wrong. Also, in W20, you can get a false positive it the target is per-change garou.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-01T12:15:12-08:00","id":1498560,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1498516-kinfolk-clarification?p=1498526#post1498526\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So, now only the small lupus population can parent lupus garou. While male homids could in theory shift into lupus form and impregnate a wolf, this is not common, due to the instincts of the wolf and the pack dynamic. Normally only one female wolf per pack would mate, and that with the dominant male wolf in the pack, not the garou who happens to come by.</div> </div> </div> </div>Reminder: the &quot;dominant&quot; wolves in a wild-living pack are the parents. The other wolves are their children - cubs and yearlings. They don't mate because they aren't equipped to do so yet, and wolves tend to avoid family for that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> While a female lupus-born can make a conscious decision to become pregnant and probably find a male wolf to mate, this simply isn't the case for wolf kinfolk. </div> </div> </div>If the kin wolf is old enough, they would be seeking out a mate to produce a family, read &quot;pack&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, wolves only carry cubs at a specific time of the year, and while they carry more cubs in a litter than an average human pregnancy, they should still be less cubs on average, due to only one female mating. </div> </div> </div>...what?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In theory all female lupus-born could be fertile in homid form all-year round, and their kids would be wolves or lupus-born, but that seems to go against instincts as well. Also, I think this is something nobody who wrote the rule into revised, thought through. </div> </div> </div>What it actually goes against is the Garou need for warriors to go and fight, and general lack of leeway with &quot;babysitting&quot;. Also, instincts are a small corner of behaviors that don't account for a lot of what any given metazoan, vertebrates in particular, lives, learns, and experiences.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In any case, garou lupus population is not easily done. </div> </div> </div>Mostly because there isn't much safe territory for wolves, which typically require a vast amount of territory for support, and also to not be hunted down with extreme prejudice by hairless apes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What is &quot;true&quot; Kinfolk, in your words?<br /> Any wolf from a litter with a garou parent is, per definition, kinfolk. They are somewhat smarter than normal wolves, possibly, but they are essentially still animals and behave accordingly. </div> </div> </div>One of the worst parts of Intelligence prior to W20 was linking it to IQ.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-02T05:21:11-08:00","id":1498616,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar23_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"23\" data-vbnamecard=\"23\">Saur Ops Specialist</a> Wolves and packs are somewhat more diverse. You are absolutely right, that normally a parent pair and their offspring are a pack. But wolves, according to wikipedia (German article), tend to leave the parent pack after 10 to 54 months of age, which is a huge span. Also, they reach the age in which they can reproduce at about two years of age in the wilderness - while in captivity they can reach it as early as 9 months after birth. There are packs that have two females and a male as their &quot;reproductive core&quot; - although that is not the norm. There are also foreign wolves accepted with the pack, occasionally, in preparation to leave the pack with one of the offspring at a later point.<br /> <br /> So, in general, there are situations where more than one pair of wolves is within a pack that could, in theory, reproduce, but usually doesn't do, due to social structure. Since they are animals, this is instinct.<br /> Litters tend to have four to six cubs, but as few as one to as many as 11 have been recorded. Average packs have five to twelve wolves, which would be the parents plus the offspring of the two previous years. Although, packs of up to 36 wolves have been recorded.<br /> <br /> Obviously the availability of sustenance plays a role when it comes to offspring leaving the pack and healthy births.<br /> <br /> So, in theory, every 10 to 54 months, in the wilderness, up 2 and a half male and female wolves (on average) start to leave a pack and go to form their own. A garou could come in and create one generation with such a wolf, but it would take some commitment, time wise. Sure, the male garou could always leave the female wolf to fend for her own, but that would diminish survival chances for mother and litter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-02T06:18:24-08:00","id":1498619,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1498516-kinfolk-clarification?p=1498616#post1498616\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar23_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"23\" data-vbnamecard=\"23\">Saur Ops Specialist</a> Wolves and packs are somewhat more diverse. You are absolutely right, that normally a parent pair and their offspring are a pack. But wolves, according to wikipedia (German article), tend to leave the parent pack after 10 to 54 months of age, which is a huge span. Also, they reach the age in which they can reproduce at about two years of age in the wilderness - while in captivity they can reach it as early as 9 months after birth. There are packs that have two females and a male as their &quot;reproductive core&quot; - although that is not the norm. There are also foreign wolves accepted with the pack, occasionally, in preparation to leave the pack with one of the offspring at a later point.</div> </div> </div> </div>Very few kin wolves are going to be captive-born, and Garou likely aren't going to rush what is already pretty speedy in comparison with the life cycle of their homid brethren.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, in general, there are situations where more than one pair of wolves is within a pack that could, in theory, reproduce, but usually doesn't do, due to social structure. Since they are animals, this is instinct. </div> </div> </div>You err here. Instincts are a specific set of behaviors. You can't just point to any behavior exhibited by an animal and say &quot;instinct&quot;. Wolves learn how to be wolves from other wolves; separated from that structure, they tend to not get along well with other wolves, so it's highly doubtful that anything involving the social structure is &quot;instinct&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Litters tend to have four to six cubs, but as few as one to as many as 11 have been recorded. Average packs have five to twelve wolves, which would be the parents plus the offspring of the two previous years. Although, packs of up to 36 wolves have been recorded.<br /> <br /> Obviously the availability of sustenance plays a role when it comes to offspring leaving the pack and healthy births.<br /> <br /> So, in theory, every 10 to 54 months, in the wilderness, up 2 and a half male and female wolves (on average) start to leave a pack and go to form their own. A garou could come in and create one generation with such a wolf, but it would take some commitment, time wise. Sure, the male garou could always leave the female wolf to fend for her own, but that would diminish survival chances for mother and litter. </div> </div> </div>A required duty that I mentioned, regardless of the exact amount of time from birth to splitting off it takes.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-02T14:13:13-08:00","id":1498637,"title":"","content":"Okay, reading up on &quot;insticts&quot; you are probably right. The term is not well defined and it appears that the implications generally made by the term's usage are often disputed in newer behavioural studies. Still, for our discussion, is the point you are trying to make that a garou could actually join a pack and re-educate the wolves so that all members of the pack who could bear offspring without inbreeding to do so?<br /> I mean it is hard to grasp for me. But, if it isn't instinct, then a group of wolves raised in captivity and separated from each other would not adhere to the behaviour once they are brought together. Would they even mate, or is that also learned behaviour, as opposed to an instinct? I don't know.<br /> <br /> <br /> As far as the garou and their duties go. There is a general problem in the whole logic of garou day-by-day affairs.<br /> In theory garou could go into the umbra in any part of the world associated with either greed, despair or what ever concept is part of the Urge-Wyrm schema. They would fight the banes and destroy their essence and could go on, every day of the week and every waking hour of the day. But is that what we think garou are actually doing? Is this how Novels and in-play fluff texts depict them?<br /> It is not. There are garou travelling, standing guard at the caern, investigating, going on quests to do this and that, form pacts with spirits, produce paraphernalia for Rites or to give as Chiminage and lots of other stuff, including mating. Sure, it was probably a guy who wrote that honourably mating was giving renown, but raising of kids didn't. Anyway, my point above was not, that a lupus female would bear as many children as possible to bring more lupus garou into the world (although peer pressure and such could create such a case), but that due to the strange rule (in my opinion) in revised, the lupus-born garou female could actually become pregnant at a time a wolf could not, biologically.<br /> <br /> There might be a difference between the Amazon's constant state of war and a sept in the most remote wilderness of Canada, in regards on how actively any werewolf is pursuing the fight against the wyrm and whether or not there is time to go through a pregnancy. That is, unless you play with the prophecy that the last cub was already born. If that is a fact, than any additional pregnancy is pointless... But I think with W20 and W5 on its way, we can all assume that this element of the Time of Judgement is not relevant in the game setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-02T15:20:49-08:00","id":1498643,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1498516-kinfolk-clarification?p=1498637#post1498637\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay, reading up on &quot;insticts&quot; you are probably right. The term is not well defined and it appears that the implications generally made by the term's usage are often disputed in newer behavioural studies. Still, for our discussion, is the point you are trying to make that a garou could actually join a pack and re-educate the wolves so that all members of the pack who could bear offspring without inbreeding to do so?<br /> I mean it is hard to grasp for me. But, if it isn't instinct, then a group of wolves raised in captivity and separated from each other would not adhere to the behaviour once they are brought together. Would they even mate, or is that also learned behaviour, as opposed to an instinct? I don't know.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's my personal axe to grind. That and the triune brain model.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As far as the garou and their duties go. There is a general problem in the whole logic of garou day-by-day affairs.<br /> In theory garou could go into the umbra in any part of the world associated with either greed, despair or what ever concept is part of the Urge-Wyrm schema. They would fight the banes and destroy their essence and could go on, every day of the week and every waking hour of the day. But is that what we think garou are actually doing? Is this how Novels and in-play fluff texts depict them?<br /> It is not. There are garou travelling, standing guard at the caern, investigating, going on quests to do this and that, form pacts with spirits, produce paraphernalia for Rites or to give as Chiminage and lots of other stuff, including mating. Sure, it was probably a guy who wrote that honourably mating was giving renown, but raising of kids didn't. Anyway, my point above was not, that a lupus female would bear as many children as possible to bring more lupus garou into the world (although peer pressure and such could create such a case), but that due to the strange rule (in my opinion) in revised, the lupus-born garou female could actually become pregnant at a time a wolf could not, biologically. </div> </div> </div>Being Garou is a pathway to many abilities that humans and wolves might consider... unnatural...<br /> <br /> (jk, it's ultranatural!)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There might be a difference between the Amazon's constant state of war and a sept in the most remote wilderness of Canada, in regards on how actively any werewolf is pursuing the fight against the wyrm and whether or not there is time to go through a pregnancy. </div> </div> </div>And the state of prey populations nearby, of course.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That is, unless you play with the prophecy that the last cub was already born. If that is a fact, than any additional pregnancy is pointless... But I think with W20 and W5 on its way, we can all assume that this element of the Time of Judgement is not relevant in the game setting. </div> </div> </div>It only kicks in when the ST wants it to, yes.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}